WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.250
You may already have heard of the
Tree That Owns Itself.

00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:04.850
It's had a Wikipedia article since 2006.

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:06.970
There are lots of news articles about it,

00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:11.160
and a Tumblr post that has, as I record this,
more than 800,000 notes.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:13.180
Not views, notes.

00:00:13.190 --> 00:00:15.679
And there are already a lot of YouTube videos
about this.

00:00:15.679 --> 00:00:17.930
Someone visits this tree in Athens, Georgia,

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:21.580
and tells the story of how a local landowner
with fond childhood memories

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:24.680
wrote a legal document bestowing
the tree's ownership onto itself

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:27.060
sometime in the early 19th century.

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:29.800
And then, they'll go into a discourse
about how, under the actual law,

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:32.860
there's no legal basis for that
so the ownership is unclear.

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:37.300
And then they'll talk about how everyone just
agrees to behave like it is the law,

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:40.920
even though this oak tree isn't even actually
the same tree?

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:43.730
The original one fell in the 1940s
and was replaced.

00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:45.840
And finally, they'll talk about how all law

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:50.980
is ultimately based on the
social contract that each of us follows.

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:54.880
What they don't talk about is
the other tree that owns itself,

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:57.420
which is about 200 miles that way.

00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:02.220
In the city of Eufaula, just over
the border into Alabama,

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:05.799
there is another oak tree
with a nearly-identical story.

00:01:05.799 --> 00:01:07.049
It was a city landmark.

00:01:07.049 --> 00:01:09.610
The local council bestowed
the tree's ownership onto itself,

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:11.409
and everyone just rolled with it.

00:01:11.409 --> 00:01:14.560
And when the tree eventually fell in a storm,
a new one was put here,

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:17.780
and everyone just agreed that it
counted as the same tree.

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:19.940
But this tree does not have a Wikipedia article.

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:22.439
It isn't even referenced on the
other tree's article,

00:01:22.439 --> 00:01:26.780
at least not as I record this.
There aren't dozens of YouTube videos about it.

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:30.460
Information about this tree has been buried
under all the search results and news articles

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:33.119
dedicated to the tree over in Georgia.

00:01:33.119 --> 00:01:36.799
For some reason,
that one has better publicity.

00:01:36.799 --> 00:01:38.950
Maybe it's because that one was first,
by about a century,

00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:42.060
but given that both of them were deeded decades
before the internet,

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:45.090
I'm not sure that's
too much of an advantage.

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:49.380
More likely, it's the same reason that many
different ideas take off while others don't:

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:52.310
it's all about who you know.

00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:56.649
The tree in Georgia is in a big college town,
home to the University of Georgia,

00:01:56.649 --> 00:02:01.299
and home to students who will arrive from
out of town, learn the story, and then leave.

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:04.220
Eufaula, here? While it's a really pretty city,

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:08.680
it has a fraction of the population and no
big reason for out-of-towners to visit.

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:11.440
Stories don't tend to spread
as well from places like this.

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:15.160
Heck, maybe there are other trees out there in other
small towns where the same thing's happened,

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:20.320
but I couldn't find any references to them under all
the noise made by the one that everyone noticed.

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:24.540
If you want a metaphor for how success on
the internet and, heck, anywhere works,

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:26.440
then these trees are it:

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:29.210
it's not always about
how good your story is.

00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:32.400
It's about how many people are
willing and able to pass it on.

